--TEST--
A payload naming a `Core` class resolves against the library's own table on the way back in, and comes back as that class with its slots intact — `rule:classes/serialize-is-a-closed-format`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A `Core\Time\Date` is an ordinary Novis object — one descriptor and three
// slots — so the copy boundary has always been able to *write* one. Reading it
// back needs a descriptor under the name the payload carries, and no compiled
// unit's class table holds a `Core` class: what answers is the process-wide
// resolver `nvs_codegen::Unit::install_in` installs, asked after the program's
// own table has been asked and has not held it.
var $day = Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 16);
var $back = Core\Serialize::decode(Core\Serialize::encode($day));

// The class name and every slot, which together are what "resolved" means
// here: a refusal would have thrown a `ParseError` naming the class instead.
// `Core\Debug::render` is how the value is read rather than a member call,
// because a program cannot yet narrow a `mixed` to a `Core` class —
// `nvs_runtime::graph`'s known gap 2 owns that half.
echo Core\Debug::render($back), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Time\Date#1 (3) {
  $year => int(2026)
  $month => int(9)
  $day => int(16)
}
